      "slug": "m1-l8-interpreting-your-first-week-baseline-and-red-flags",
      "title": "Interpreting Your First Week: Baseline and Red Flags",
      "section": "Module 1: Foundations of Health Metrics",
      "body": "# Module 1, Foundations of Health Metrics, Lesson 8: Interpreting Your First Week: Baseline and Red Flags\n\nGoal: By the end of this lesson you can read your first seven days of data, set your own personal baseline instead of comparing yourself to other people, and name the three patterns worth a closer look.\n\n## Quick recall\n Last time we covered why small, data-guided changes beat guessing. Two quick questions.\nOne: what works better, tracking your numbers and making tiny adjustments, or just guessing how you feel? Tracking and adjusting. Guessing leaves you flying blind.\nTwo: why do small changes matter so much? Because tiny consistent changes compound. They stack up over weeks into something big. Hold onto that. Today you collect the first numbers those changes will work on.\n\n## Why this matters\n Here is the mistake almost everyone makes on day one. They look at their resting heart rate, see seventy beats a minute, then look up \"normal\" online, see sixty to one hundred, and think, \"Okay, I'm fine, nothing to learn here.\" That comparison is the wrong move. The most useful number in your whole first week is not how you compare to a stranger. It is how you compare to yourself.\n\n## The idea\n Your first seven days have one job. You are learning what is normal for you. We call that your starting point (your baseline). Everyone's baseline is different. A study of more than ninety thousand adults found that a normal resting heart rate can differ by as much as seventy beats a minute from one healthy person to the next (Quer et al., 2020). So a friend's number tells you almost nothing. Your own number, measured over a week, tells you everything.\n\nOnce you have a few days of data, look for patterns. Two are common. First, does Monday stress show up in your heart rate? Many people see their resting heart rate climb at the start of the work week. Second, does moving more help you sleep? Watch for nights after you walked more than about seven thousand steps. Many people sleep better on those nights. You are looking for your own cause and effect.\n\n## Picture it\n Think of your first week like learning a new song on the radio. The first time through, it is just noise. By the seventh day, you start to hum along. You know where the loud parts come and where it goes quiet. Your body's data is the same. One day is noise. Seven days is a tune you can follow.\n\n## Watch out\n Here is what trips people up. They panic at a single bad number. One night of poor sleep, one high heart rate reading, and they think something is wrong. It usually is not. In that study of ninety thousand adults, about one in five people had a week where their resting heart rate jumped by ten beats or more, and they were healthy (Quer et al., 2020). One number is normal noise. So when do you actually pay attention? Three things. One: your resting heart rate sits about ten beats above your normal for several days in a row, not just one morning. Two: your sleep efficiency, the share of time in bed you actually spend asleep, drops below eighty percent. Most healthy people stay above ninety (Reed & Sacco, 2016). Three: a sharp drop in your daily activity that lasts more than three days. None of these means alarm. They mean pay attention, and ask what changed.\n\n## Quick check\n Quick check. Your resting heart rate is usually about sixty-two. This morning it reads seventy. Should you worry? No. One morning above your normal is just noise. You would only pay attention if it stayed about ten beats high for several days in a row. One reading is a snapshot, not a pattern.\n\n## Key Takeaways\n- Your first seven days are for setting your own baseline, not comparing to other people. A normal resting heart rate can differ by up to seventy beats a minute between healthy people (Quer et al., 2020).\n- One off number is normal variation. About one in five healthy adults has a week where resting heart rate jumps ten beats or more (Quer et al., 2020).\n- Look for patterns: does stress raise your heart rate on Mondays, and do you sleep better after walking more than about seven thousand steps.\n- Red flag one: resting heart rate about ten beats above your normal for several days in a row.\n- Red flag two: sleep efficiency below eighty percent (most healthy people stay above ninety percent) (Reed & Sacco, 2016). Red flag three: a sharp activity drop lasting more than three days. Red flags mean pay attention, not panic.\n\n## Sources\n- Quer, G., et al. (2020). Inter- and intraindividual variability in daily resting heart rate and its associations with age, sex, sleep, BMI, and time of year: Retrospective, longitudinal cohort study of 92,457 adults. (About one in five had a week with resting heart rate fluctuating ten or more beats a minute; individual normal ranges can differ by up to seventy beats a minute between people.)\n- Reed, D. L., & Sacco, W. P. (2016). Measuring sleep efficiency: What should the denominator be? Journal of Clinical Sleep Medicine. (Sleep efficiency of about eighty percent is the threshold for normal; most healthy young adults exceed ninety percent.)"
