      "body": "# Module 5, Long-Term Trends and Sustainable Systems, Lesson 10: The Capstone: My Health Blueprint\n\nGoal: By the end of this lesson you can build a one-page Health Blueprint from your five weeks of data.\n\n## Quick recall\n Last time we covered community and health leadership. Two quick questions.\n\nOne: what does sharing your habits and supporting other people build? Accountability. When someone is watching for your update, you show up.\n\nTwo: why does that accountability matter for tracking? Because it helps you stick with it. A habit you talk about out loud is a habit you keep.\n\n## Why this matters\n Picture five weeks of numbers sitting in your tracker app right now. Steps. Resting heart rate. Sleep. Maybe a little experiment you ran. All of it is just sitting there. Today you turn that pile of data into one page you can read in thirty seconds. That page is your Health Blueprint. It is the whole point of these five weeks.\n\n## The idea\n A Health Blueprint is a short, written summary of where you are and where you are going. It has four parts, and you build them in order.\n\nPart one. Pull your data. Open your tracker app and look at the past five weeks. Write down three or four numbers you trust most. Your average daily steps. Your resting heart rate. Your typical sleep hours. These are your numbers, not anyone else's.\n\nPart two. Summarize what changed. Compare week one to week five. Did your steps go up? Did your resting heart rate drop? Write one or two plain sentences. Something like, \"My steps went from four thousand to six thousand, and my resting heart rate dropped three beats.\" That is it.\n\nPart three. Set three goals for the next ninety days (a quarter of a year). Three is the number. Not ten. Make each one specific. \"Walk seven thousand steps on most days\" beats \"be more active.\" Research backs that step target. Middle-aged adults who took about seven thousand or more steps a day had roughly fifty to seventy percent lower death risk than people under seven thousand (Paluch et al., 2021).\n\nPart four. Pick one habit to keep. Just one. The single thing from these five weeks that you will not drop. Maybe it is a morning walk. Maybe it is checking your sleep before bed. One habit, protected.\n\n## Picture it\n Think of a blueprint for a house. It is not the house. It is one clear page that shows where every wall goes, so the builders know what to do next. Your Health Blueprint works the same way. One page. It tells the future version of you where the walls go.\n\n## Watch out\n Here is the common mistake. People try to set ten goals and track twelve metrics, because more feels better. It is not. A blueprint with ten goals is a blueprint nobody follows. The power is in cutting down. Three goals. One habit. A few trusted numbers. If your blueprint does not fit on one page, you have too much on it. Cut until it fits.\n\n## Quick check\n Quick check. How many ninety-day goals does your Health Blueprint set, and how many habits do you commit to keep? Three goals and one habit. Why? Because a short blueprint is one you will actually use, and three goals is enough to make real progress without burning out.\n\n## Key Takeaways\n- A Health Blueprint is a one-page summary built in four steps: pull your data, summarize what changed, set goals, pick one habit.\n- Pull three or four numbers you trust from your past five weeks, like average steps, resting heart rate, and sleep hours.\n- Set exactly three goals for the next ninety days, and make each one specific.\n- Pick exactly one habit to keep, the one thing you will not drop.\n- Middle-aged adults taking about seven thousand or more steps a day had roughly fifty to seventy percent lower death risk than those under seven thousand (Paluch et al., 2021).\n\n## Sources\n- Paluch and colleagues, two thousand twenty-one, a study of middle-aged adults showing that about seven thousand or more steps a day was linked to a large drop in death risk compared with fewer than seven thousand."
